1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.703
In this
lesson, we'll build an agent from scratch.

2
00:00:04.004 --> 00:00:07.307
You'll see that all agents can do fairly
complex tasks.

3
00:00:07.741 --> 00:00:10.744
A basic agent is actually not that hard
to build.

4
00:00:11.311 --> 00:00:12.712
As we're building this agent,

5
00:00:12.712 --> 00:00:15.882
it will be useful to notice
what jobs fall to the LLM

6
00:00:16.316 --> 00:00:19.319
and which are managed by the code
around the LLM.

7
00:00:19.352 --> 00:00:21.221
What we call the runtime.

8
00:00:21.221 --> 00:00:24.224
Let's dive in.

9
00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:25.658
The agent we're going to build from

10
00:00:25.658 --> 00:00:28.661
scratch is based on the ReAct pattern.

11
00:00:28.828 --> 00:00:32.298
ReAct stands for reasoning plus acting.

12
00:00:32.665 --> 00:00:36.069
So in this pattern, an LLM first thinks

13
00:00:36.069 --> 00:00:39.739
about what to do
and then decides an action to take.

14
00:00:39.939 --> 00:00:43.076
That action is
then executed in an environment

15
00:00:43.276 --> 00:00:46.913
and an observation is returned.
With that observation,

16
00:00:47.213 --> 00:00:48.982
The LLM then repeats.

17
00:00:48.982 --> 00:00:52.552
So, then thinks about what to do again,
decides another action

18
00:00:52.552 --> 00:00:56.456
to take, and continues
until it decides that it is done.

19
00:00:57.724 --> 00:00:59.459
The code that we will be using to start

20
00:00:59.459 --> 00:01:03.063
with, is based on this blog post from Simon
Willison.

21
00:01:03.296 --> 00:01:06.266
It's a great introduction
to implementing the ReAct

22
00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:09.269
pattern in Python.

23
00:01:09.469 --> 00:01:12.472
To get started,
let's import everything we need.

24
00:01:13.673 --> 00:01:16.376
We can then initialize the language model.

25
00:01:16.376 --> 00:01:19.379
For this, we will use OpenAI.

26
00:01:21.481 --> 00:01:22.682
Let's try it out once

27
00:01:22.682 --> 00:01:25.685
just to make sure that it's working.

28
00:01:35.328 --> 00:01:38.064
We can see that
we pass in hello, world and get back.

29
00:01:38.064 --> 00:01:40.200
"Hello. How can I assist you today?"

30
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:40.800
Great.

31
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.470
We're set up and running with our language
model.

32
00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:46.239
Let's now create the agent.

33
00:01:46.239 --> 00:01:49.242
We'll start
by creating a class for the agent.

34
00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:54.347
We'll want this agent to be parameterized
by a system message.

35
00:01:54.948 --> 00:01:57.550
So we'll allow the user to pass that in.

36
00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:59.419
We'll then save that as an attribute.

37
00:02:00.653 --> 00:02:01.154
We'll also

38
00:02:01.154 --> 00:02:04.157
keep track
of a list of messages over time.

39
00:02:04.224 --> 00:02:06.926
This is where we will append
everything that happens

40
00:02:06.926 --> 00:02:09.929
in that ReAct loop.

41
00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.466
And to start it off,
if there is this system message,

42
00:02:13.466 --> 00:02:16.970
we're going to append a system message
in this list of messages.

43
00:02:19.038 --> 00:02:19.806
Okay great.

44
00:02:19.806 --> 00:02:22.142
So our agent can now be initialized.

45
00:02:22.142 --> 00:02:24.944
But what exactly is it going to do?

46
00:02:24.944 --> 00:02:27.947
Let's implement
the call method for this agent.

47
00:02:31.351 --> 00:02:33.419
What we want
this method to do at a high level

48
00:02:33.419 --> 00:02:36.422
is to take a message that comes
in, that is a string,

49
00:02:36.523 --> 00:02:40.160
and then append that message
to the existing array of messages.

50
00:02:41.094 --> 00:02:44.797
We'll then execute a function
will implement that right down below,

51
00:02:45.431 --> 00:02:49.202
and we'll then take the result of that
and add a new message

52
00:02:49.202 --> 00:02:50.904
into the array of messages.

53
00:02:50.904 --> 00:02:53.006
This time, coming from the assistant.

54
00:02:54.107 --> 00:02:56.809
So let's implement that execute method.

55
00:02:58.711 --> 00:02:59.913
In the execute method.

56
00:02:59.913 --> 00:03:03.750
this is where we call the OpenAI client
that we initialized above.

57
00:03:04.017 --> 00:03:06.119
So we'll be using this GPT-4 model.

58
00:03:06.119 --> 00:03:08.188
We'll be setting the temperature
equals to zero.

59
00:03:08.188 --> 00:03:09.923
This will make it very deterministic.

60
00:03:09.923 --> 00:03:13.059
And we'll pass in this list of messages
that will accumulate.

61
00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:16.129
We'll then have this method return
the content

62
00:03:16.129 --> 00:03:19.132
or the string of the message
that it gets back from the model.

63
00:03:19.699 --> 00:03:21.701
So, this is our agent.

64
00:03:21.701 --> 00:03:23.002
Awesome.

65
00:03:23.002 --> 00:03:25.772
Now let's get started
with creating the ReAct agent.

66
00:03:25.772 --> 00:03:29.876
The ReAct agent is going to require
a very specific system message.

67
00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:31.744
So let's go ahead and write that out.

68
00:03:31.744 --> 00:03:35.215
And we can take a look at that in detail
